## Isolation Levels and What They Prevent

| Level | Dirty Read | Non-Repeatable Read | Phantom Read |
|-------|-----------|---------------------|---------------|
| READ UNCOMMITTED | Possible | Possible | Possible |
| READ COMMITTED | Prevented | Possible | Possible |
| REPEATABLE READ | Prevented | Prevented | Possible |
| SERIALIZABLE | Prevented | Prevented | Prevented |

## Anomaly Definitions

- **Dirty read**: reading uncommitted data from another transaction
- **Non-repeatable read**: reading same row twice, getting different values
- **Phantom read**: running same range query twice, getting different rows

## Spring @Transactional

```java
// Default: inherits database isolation (READ_COMMITTED for PostgreSQL)
@Transactional
public void transfer(Long from, Long to, BigDecimal amount) {
    accountRepo.debit(from, amount);
    accountRepo.credit(to, amount);
    // Exception → both roll back (Atomicity)
}

// Upgrade for reports needing consistent snapshot
@Transactional(isolation = Isolation.REPEATABLE_READ)
public FinancialReport buildReport(LocalDate date) {
    // All reads within this transaction see consistent data
    BigDecimal revenue = revenueRepo.sumByDate(date);
    BigDecimal costs   = costRepo.sumByDate(date);
    return new FinancialReport(revenue, costs);
}
```

## MVCC — How PostgreSQL Achieves Isolation

PostgreSQL uses Multi-Version Concurrency Control: instead of locking rows, it keeps old versions of rows. Readers see a consistent snapshot without blocking writers. Writers create new versions without blocking readers. This is why PostgreSQL can handle high read/write concurrency without readers and writers constantly blocking each other.

## Views

```sql
-- Regular view: re-executes every query against it
CREATE VIEW active_premium_users AS
SELECT u.id, u.name, u.email, s.plan, s.expires_at
FROM users u
JOIN subscriptions s ON u.id = s.user_id
WHERE s.status = 'active' AND s.plan = 'premium';

-- Application code: just query the view
SELECT * FROM active_premium_users WHERE expires_at < NOW() + INTERVAL '7 days';
```

## Materialized Views

```sql
-- Stores the result on disk — fast reads, must manually refresh
CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW monthly_revenue_summary AS
SELECT DATE_TRUNC('month', created_at) AS month,
       SUM(amount) AS total_revenue,
       COUNT(*) AS order_count
FROM orders
WHERE status = 'completed'
GROUP BY 1;

CREATE INDEX ON monthly_revenue_summary (month);

-- Refresh on a schedule (cron or Spring @Scheduled)
REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY monthly_revenue_summary;
-- CONCURRENTLY allows reads during refresh (no table lock)
```

## Trigger Example (Audit Log)

```sql
CREATE FUNCTION log_order_changes() RETURNS TRIGGER AS $$
BEGIN
    INSERT INTO order_audit_log (order_id, old_status, new_status, changed_at)
    VALUES (NEW.id, OLD.status, NEW.status, NOW());
    RETURN NEW;
END;
$$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;

CREATE TRIGGER order_status_audit
AFTER UPDATE OF status ON orders
FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION log_order_changes();
```

## Spring: Refresh Materialized View

```java
@Scheduled(cron = "0 0 * * * *")  // every hour
@Transactional
public void refreshRevenueSummary() {
    jdbcTemplate.execute("REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY monthly_revenue_summary");
}
```
